# Authentication (Better Auth)

The starter ships local email/password sign-in and self-registration. It is
not a complete authentication system: password reset, email verification, and
OAuth are deliberately deferred (see below). Two defaults are already safe:
`/admin` admits only accounts holding the `admin` role, which no sign-up can
grant, and `POST /api/login` and `POST /api/register` are throttled per email
and client address by the `auth` plugin's default limiter.

## One-time setup

These are the steps of "Getting started" in `AGENTS.md`, in the same order;
`jsails create` printed the same list.

1. Start the git-ignored `.env` from the committed example. Every `jsails`
   command loads `.env` (then `.env.local` and `.env.<NODE_ENV>`), and the
   `auth:migrate` / `user:create` npm scripts load `.env` and `.env.local`
   through Node's `--env-file-if-exists`. A real environment variable always
   wins over the files, so exporting them in your shell or process manager
   works too.

   ```sh
   cp .env.auth.example .env
   ```

2. Fill in every value the example leaves blank. Better Auth requires a secret
   of at least 32 bytes, and a live server-component render outside
   development and test needs `JSAILS_COMPONENT_SECRET` (also at least 32
   bytes). `jamal up` creates the MariaDB user and database from the
   `DATABASE_*` values:

   ```sh
   BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=...        # openssl rand -base64 32
   JSAILS_COMPONENT_SECRET=...   # openssl rand -base64 32
   DATABASE_HOST=127.0.0.1
   DATABASE_PORT=3306            # default, optional
   DATABASE_USER=...
   DATABASE_PASSWORD=...
   DATABASE_NAME=...
   DATABASE_ROOT_PASSWORD=...
   ```

3. Start MariaDB and Valkey locally (Docker). Auth requires a running
   database connection:

   ```sh
   npx jsails jamal up
   ```

4. Build the app; `db:push` reads the compiled model registry:

   ```sh
   npm run build
   ```

5. Create the framework, plugin, and model tables, then Better Auth's own
   tables (`auth:migrate` owns those; `db:push` leaves them alone):

   ```sh
   npm run db:push
   npm run auth:migrate
   ```

6. Create the first admin. `name` is optional; email and password are
   required, and `--role admin` grants the role the admin panel checks:

   ```sh
   npm run user:create -- you@example.com 'a long password' "Your Name" --role admin
   ```

   Without `--role` the script creates an ordinary account, the same as one
   registered at `/register`. If the email already has an account (you
   registered at `/register` first, or an earlier run failed after creating
   it), rerun with only the email and `--role admin`; the script assigns the
   role to that account and leaves its password unchanged:

   ```sh
   npm run user:create -- you@example.com --role admin
   ```

7. Run the app at http://localhost:3000:

   ```sh
   npm run dev
   ```

Visit `/login` to sign in and `/dashboard` to see the signed-in state.

## How it fits together

- Better Auth owns its tables in a MariaDB database you point it at through the
  `DATABASE_*` variables (`DATABASE_HOST`, `DATABASE_PORT`, `DATABASE_USER`,
  `DATABASE_PASSWORD`, `DATABASE_NAME`), a separate schema from any application
  data you add later. `BETTER_AUTH_SECRET` signs its sessions. Keep the
  credentials out of version control.
- `/api/login` and `/api/logout` are plain form targets: the pages post to them
  and the handlers call Better Auth server-side. There is no browser SDK and no
  secret in the client bundle. They are mounted as trusted extension hook routes
  (see `jsails.app.js`), not filesystem API routes, so they bypass the
  filesystem default-deny `authorize` and the session CSRF middleware — a plain
  form POST works even while a session cookie is present.
- Each of those handlers owns its own same-origin check: the request's `Origin`
  must match the origin the request was addressed to (`server.origin`, or a
  tenant subdomain in `server.hosts`), and a mismatch is rejected with `403` before any
  credentials are read.
- `POST /api/login` is throttled by the `auth` plugin's default limiter
  (`createMemoryRateLimiter`): 10 attempts per route, email, and client address
  and 100 per client address across all emails in a 15-minute window, answered
  with a generic `429`. Keying on the address too means a stranger cannot lock
  your account out, and the per-address bucket stops one client spraying a
  password over many accounts. Registration gets the same limiter with its own
  counters, and so would password reset once enabled. Its counters live in process
  memory: with more than one app process, pass a shared (Valkey-backed) hook as
  `login.rateLimit`, `registration.rateLimit`, and `passwordReset.rateLimit`.
  Behind a reverse proxy, `JSAILS_TRUSTED_PROXIES` must name the proxies in
  front of the app (`hops=<n>` or their CIDR ranges) so the limiter keys on the
  client rather than the proxy. The generated `jamal.config.ts` sets `hops=1`
  for kamal-proxy on the `jamal deploy` path; raise it there if you put another
  proxy in front. The
  client address is the X-Forwarded-For entry the outermost trusted proxy
  appended, never the client-controlled left-most one, and an IPv6 client is
  keyed on its /64.
- `/register` posts to `POST /api/register` (the plugin's
  `registration: { enabled: true }`). The account is created without a session,
  so the new user then signs in at `/login`. The address is not verified.
- The admin panel at `/admin` uses `adminOnly` from `@jsails/auth`: it admits
  only a session whose user holds the `admin` role. The role is a column on
  Better Auth's user table that no sign-up or profile endpoint can write;
  `setUserRole(auth, email, role)` assigns or clears it, and
  `npm run user:create -- <email> [password] --role admin` calls it, for a new
  account or for one that already exists.
- Sessions resolve through the `auth` plugin's own Better Auth instance (the
  `resolveSession` seam in `jsails.app.js` reads the plugin's session
  service), so the app holds one auth connection pool.
- In production set `PUBLIC_ORIGIN` to the public HTTPS origin.
  `jamal.config.ts` forwards it as both `PUBLIC_ORIGIN` and `BETTER_AUTH_URL`;
  Better Auth only marks its session cookie `Secure` when that URL is https.
- `/api/me` returns the current user and is the one protected filesystem API:
  the app's global `authorize` is default-deny, so every filesystem API request
  is rejected unless a session is present. Pages are not covered by `authorize`,
  so `/dashboard` renders a signed-out prompt when the session is absent.
- Login/logout own their origin checks, but they are trusted hook routes and do
  not set a precedent: any authenticated filesystem mutation you add
  (`POST`/`PUT`/`PATCH`/`DELETE` under `api/`) still runs the framework CSRF
  pipeline, which requires a same-origin `Origin` and a matching `X-CSRF-Token`
  for cookie-authenticated requests. Supply `context.session.csrfToken` as that
  header.

## CLI sign-in with `jsails login` (browser-approved)

The same repository builds the web app **and** a small CLI: `commands/*` modules
compile into `dist/commands/` and are run with `jsails <name>`. Three commands
ship:

```sh
jsails login      # request a device code and approve it in the browser
jsails whoami     # print the signed-in account (name and email)
jsails logout     # delete the stored CLI session credentials
```

`jsails login` runs the OAuth 2.0 Device Authorization Grant (RFC 8628) against
the app's Better Auth device endpoints. It requests a code, prints a short user
code and a verification URL, opens the browser for you (pass `--no-open` to
skip), and polls until you approve. Approve it on the `/device` page — the same
page a browser user reaches after choosing "Sign in from a device" on `/login` —
by confirming the user code while signed in. The CLI's base URL comes from
`APP_URL`, then `BETTER_AUTH_URL`, then `http://localhost:3000`.

The resulting access token is a Better Auth **session token** (not a separate
credential), stored on disk so later `jsails whoami` / `jsails logout` runs can
use it:

- **Location:** `${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-~/.config}/jsails/<appName>/credentials.json`,
  where `<appName>` is the package name (or the directory basename when no
  `package.json` is present). The directory is created `0700` and the file is
  written atomically (temp file + rename) with mode `0600`, so other local
  users cannot read the token.
- **Shape:** `{ baseUrl, token, tokenType, expiresAt, scope }`. The token is
  never printed by the CLI.

### Security notes

- The device flow is Better Auth's official RFC 8628 implementation. The
  `client_id` (`jsails-cli`) must match the server's `validateClient` allowlist,
  and approval requires an authenticated session on `/device` — the CLI never
  sees the user's password.
- Tokens are session tokens: revoke one by clearing/rotating its session
  server-side (Better Auth session revocation), and remove the CLI-side copy
  with `jsails logout`. Re-running `jsails login` simply overwrites the
  credentials file.
- The token is stored in plain text on disk (mode `0600`). Treat the account the
  same as a saved browser session, and run `jsails logout` on shared machines.

## Deliberately deferred

- Password reset and email verification. The plugin mounts them with
  `passwordReset: { enabled: true }` / `verification: { enabled: true }` and a
  `sendMail` callback (`sendMail` from `@jsails/auth` sends through the
  `@jsails/mail` plugin, which needs a real transport outside development and
  test). The starter ships no reset page, because the plugin requests the
  reset without a redirect target: the emailed link cannot yet land on an app
  page that reads its `token` and posts it with the new password to
  `POST /api/password-reset/confirm`.
- OAuth / social sign-in.
- Finer-grained roles — the starter checks only `admin`; `requireRole(...)`
  from `@jsails/auth` gates on any set of role names.
- Production hardening — a shared multi-process rate limiter, account lockout,
  and audit logging are not wired.
